# Skill install kits. Three record types, whitespace separated:
#   kit <id> <description>     declares a kit; the description is what an
#                              installer, a plugin manifest, or a picker shows
#   explicit <id>              marks a declared kit explicit: never offered by
#                              the interactive picker, excluded from --all, and
#                              installed ONLY via a literal --with <id>. When an
#                              explicit kit is not selected, the installer
#                              REMOVES its previously installed artifacts —
#                              presence without recorded selection is not
#                              consent for a consent-gated kit.
#   <skill> <kit>              puts a skill in a declared kit
# A skill with no record belongs to `core`, which always installs, and may name
# only one kit. Keep LF line endings: the bash reader rejects CRLF loudly,
# the TypeScript reader trims and tolerates it — they only agree on LF.
# Adding a kit is a `kit` line plus its membership lines — nothing else.

kit core Enforcement, planning, and workflow skills for everyday use
kit product Product-model skills: evidence-backed briefs and product review
kit adversarial-review Adversarial review: independent refutation lane + evidence records + hard merge gate (explicit opt-in)
kit advisory-review Advisory review: one non-authoring reviewer pass per substantive change, as an always-loaded instruction (explicit opt-in)
explicit adversarial-review
explicit advisory-review

product-intelligence product
product-review product
adversarial-review adversarial-review
